{{- /*
  Launcher stubs in ~/.local/bin that must not exist.

  Each one is four lines of the same shape (verified identical across all 13):

      #!/bin/bash
      export MISE_MINIMUM_RELEASE_AGE=0
      mise use -g --quiet "<tool>" || exit 1
      exec mise x "<tool>" -- "<tool>" "$@"

  `mise use -g` WRITES ~/.config/mise/config.toml, which is a chezmoi-managed
  file. So every single invocation of any of these stubs causes chezmoi drift --
  the tool "works", and silently rewrites managed state behind chezmoi's back.

  ~/.local/bin also sits ahead of the system directories on PATH during the
  bootstrap (run_onchange_after_install_mise_toolchain.sh.tmpl), which is what
  made the 2026-08-27 incident reachable: mise's github credential_command ran
  a bare `gh`, PATH found this stub, the stub re-entered mise, mise needed a
  token again -- ~30k tasks and 15GB of RAM in four minutes. That setting is now
  gone (see dot_config/mise/config.toml.tmpl), but the stubs are an independent
  bug and all 13 share the drift problem, so all 13 go.

  Safe to delete: PATH resolves the real binaries first. e.g. `type -a claude`
  finds ~/.local/share/mise/installs/claude/latest/claude ahead of the stub.
  Nothing in this repo references ~/.local/bin except PATH exports, and
  ~/.local/bin/mise (used by README.md:40, tests/bats/helpers/common.bash:4 and
  tests/podman/Containerfile.arch:45-46) is deliberately NOT listed here.

  WARNING -- .chezmoiremove is NOT idempotent once a target is RECREATED.
  Verified on chezmoi v2.72.0: the first apply deletes silently, but if a stub
  comes back, the next apply prompts interactively ("... has changed since
  chezmoi last wrote it") and HANGS even under --no-tty. Any non-interactive
  apply path -- topgrade, cron, the podman harness -- must pass --force.
*/ -}}
.local/bin/claude
.local/bin/codex
.local/bin/copilot
.local/bin/crush
.local/bin/gemini
.local/bin/gh
.local/bin/ghui
.local/bin/grok
.local/bin/hunk
.local/bin/omp
.local/bin/opencode
.local/bin/pi
.local/bin/playwright
